  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B059",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B047",
    "B045"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the strongest present case because the magic-wavelength lattice is a sharply identifiable physical idea that enabled long interrogation of many atoms while controlling motion-related shifts. Katori and Ye form a compact, complementary attribution for the central invention and its decisive experimental realization. The achievement has been independently reproduced at extraordinary performance and has matured into a reliable measurement capability for frequency metrology, gravitation, and tests of fundamental physics. This is sufficient on its demonstrated physics; a future redefinition of the second is neither necessary nor an appropriate substitute for that evidence.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation remains historical attribution. Optical lattice clocks rest on important advances in lasers, cooling, atomic control, and frequency metrology, and a priority review must establish that Katori and Ye capture the decisive advances rather than merely prominent later performance work. The citation must remain confined to invention and realization of optical lattice clocks, not the whole development of optical frequency standards.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce considers IceCube a mature opening of high-energy neutrino astronomy but stresses that three names may inadequately represent a very large collaboration.",
        "response": "I accept the exceptional scientific significance and observational maturity of IceCube. Its collaboration-scale attribution is nevertheless more than a formal imperfection: without a documented basis for assigning conception, construction, calibration, and scientific realization to the proposed trio, the individual award remains less defensible than the bounded Katori-Ye configuration."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that aberration-corrected electron optics made dependable sub-angstrom microscopy possible and that Rose, Urban, and Haider form a chain from concepts to working instruments.",
        "response": "B047 remains a leading alternative because it plainly transformed what can be observed in materials. I remain persuaded, however, that its division of credit among foundational optics, engineering realization, validation, and parallel developments needs firmer archival reconstruction. B059 offers comparably mature experimental physics with a more sharply delimited central idea and attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors KPZ because its universal framework has exact theoretical depth and Takeuchi's experiments provide unusually clean quantitative verification.",
        "response": "The theory-to-measurement structure of KPZ is compelling, and its citation can be narrowly framed around a universality class. My preference remains lattice clocks because their independently reproducible operation has created a new, exceptionally precise experimental regime with broad fundamental reach, while retaining a particularly clear conceptual and experimental core."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a detailed priority review confirms that Katori and Ye are the complete appropriate attribution for the original optical-lattice-clock achievement.",
      "How to distinguish the magic-wavelength lattice-clock invention precisely from enabling advances and later improvements in the broader optical-clock field.",
      "For B047, whether archival evidence supports the proposed allocation among Rose, Urban, Haider, and other essential contributors."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. I have WACQT institutional links overlapping with Göran Johansson's Chalmers environment, but no connection to the proposed achievement or laureates is stated."
  }
}
